<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Possible&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;HIDE</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I have loved this concept ever since I saw the original draft, and its execution is basically perfect. It gives away just enough information for a reader to work out what exactly is going on without spelling it out for them. That being said, I have one single phrase that I'm not the biggest fan of:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>telescopes with a resolution of 0.02 arc-seconds or less.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Something about this line just rubs me the wrong way. I can't quite put my finger on what it is, it's almost definitely not even grammatically incorrect. I just much prefer phrases like 'telescopes with a resolution of 0.02 or fewer arcseconds<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-655554-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-655554-1')">1</a></sup>'</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-655554-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-655554-1')">1</a>. Also, how is arcsecond meant to be spelled? Wikipedia and several other sources say arcsecond and arc second are both acceptable, but spellcheck insists that it's hyphenated. Weird.</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
